Missing Marks Management System – Proprietary License

PROPOSED DRAFT — NOT APPROVED OR EFFECTIVE

Proposed licensing model: Proprietary — All Rights Reserved.

This document is a proposed licensing policy for the Missing Marks Management
System (the "Software"). It must be reviewed and expressly agreed to by all
relevant lawful copyright holders before it is adopted or represented as the
Software's operative license. Until that review and agreement occur, this
draft does not grant rights on behalf of any person or institution.

1. Copyright and ownership

Copyright and other intellectual property rights in the Software remain with
their respective lawful owners. This document does not identify, create, or
transfer ownership, and does not assert that any one person or institution
owns the Software exclusively. Rights in third-party materials remain subject
to their respective owners and applicable terms.

2. Limited permission to view

The Software is publicly visible on GitHub. You may access and view the
publicly available source code using GitHub's ordinary viewing functionality.
Public visibility, access to the repository, and this limited permission to
view do not grant a general license to use or reuse the Software.

3. All rights reserved; no general license

Except for the limited permission to view above and rights that cannot be
restricted under applicable law, no permission or license is granted to use,
copy, reproduce, modify, adapt, create derivative works from, publish,
distribute, sublicense, make available, or commercially exploit the Software
or any part of it.

Redistribution of the Software, in whole or in part, and creation or
distribution of derivative works require prior written authorization from all
relevant lawful rights holders. Commercial use also requires prior written
authorization from all relevant lawful rights holders. No authorization is
effective unless it is given in writing by persons or institutions with
authority to grant the relevant rights.

4. Institutional requests

Universities and other institutions may request permission to use the Software
by seeking written authorization from the relevant lawful rights holders.
Submitting a request does not itself grant permission. No contact details are
specified here because an authorized contact has not been confirmed.

5. No implied rights

No rights are granted by implication, estoppel, public availability, or any
other means except as expressly stated in an authorization from the relevant
lawful rights holders. Any rights that cannot be restricted by law remain
unaffected.

6. Ownership review required before publication

Before adopting this proposal, the project must confirm who holds rights in
the contributions and whether each relevant holder has authority to approve
these terms. Review should include the project's university or institutional
intellectual-property policies, all contributors and any contribution or
assignment agreements, work created in the course of employment or study,
and third-party code, assets, and other materials. Git history and repository
ownership information alone do not establish exclusive ownership or authority
to license every contribution.
